Folio 62v holds only prose: no formulas, equations or computations. The whole sheet is written upside down relative to the scan, in faint pencil, with heavy deletions and interlinear insertions. Only scattered words can be made out with any confidence: 'Ausdehnung', 'Begränzung', 'Fläche', 'zusammenhängend', 'Begränzungstheile', 'Theil', 'in folgender ...'. There is also a single isolated line near the top of the inverted text, roughly '... in folgender ... aufstellen'.

Context places the page. The previous page (62r) is a draft of the dissertation's opening sections. It argues that a function of z cannot be constant along a line without being constant everywhere, and it restricts discontinuities to lines and points, removable discontinuities excluded. Its references to 'Art. 15' and to the surface T match the 1851 Göttingen dissertation, 'Grundlagen für eine allgemeine Theorie der Functionen einer veränderlichen complexen Grösse'. The next page (63r) is a draft of that dissertation's introduction, on why imaginary quantities were introduced.

The legible vocabulary therefore points to a draft of the topological articles on the surface T, corresponding to Art. 5–6 of the dissertation. Those articles cover:
- the extension ('Ausdehnung') of a surface spread over the z-plane;
- its boundary ('Begränzung') and boundary pieces ('Begränzungstheile');
- connected versus simply connected ('einfach zusammenhängend') surfaces;
- cutting the surface by cross-cuts (Querschnitte) into simply connected pieces.
The published result is that a surface of connectivity order n becomes simply connected after n−1 cross-cuts, and that each non-separating cross-cut lowers the order by one.

Whether this sheet states that result cannot be confirmed from the legible words; this identification is mine and rests on vocabulary plus context. There is nothing to reconstruct step by step: the page states (as far as legible) definitions and qualitative statements only.
